[620/627] run frames_test_00813
  -> success | pred='**The Dyatlov Pass Incident (1959):**\nIn February 1959, nine experienced Soviet ski hikers from the Ural Polytechnical Institute, led by Igor Dyatlov, died under mysterious circumstances on the eastern slopes of Kholat Syakhl mountain in the northern Ural Mountains. Overnight, the group cut their way out of their tent from the inside and fled into extreme cold (as low as −40 °C), inadequately dressed. Six died of hypothermia; three died from severe physical trauma. Their bodies showed bizarre injuries, and Soviet authorities gave only a vague explanation — that the hikers had succumbed to an "unknown compelling force." The case remained officially unsolved for decades; a 2020 reinvestigation concluded an avalanche or snow slab event was the most likely cause.\n\n**Devil\'s Pass (2013) and its inspiration:**\nDevil\'s Pass (also known as The Dyatlov Pass Incident), directed by Renny Harlin and released August 23, 2013, is a found-footage horror film directly inspired by the real incident. The plot follows five American college students who travel to Russia to investigate what happened to the nine hikers in 1959. They discover a hidden Soviet bunker in the area that locks from the outside and contains classified files referencing teleportation experiments and the Philadelphia Experiment. The film takes a sci-fi/horror turn: the students encounter mutants capable of teleportation and are pulled through a wormhole that sends them back in time to 1959. The film ends with Holly and Jensen arriving in that year, where Soviet soldiers find their bodies near the bunker — implying that the original Dyatlov Pass victims may have been these future-displaced students. Director Renny Harlin\'s guiding theory was that a secret Soviet government experiment went wrong, which he used to explain the real incident\'s mysterious circumstances.'
